  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Is Replacing Your Shower Drain and Mixing Valve Necessary?",
    "text": "Is Replacing Your Shower Drain and Mixing Valve Necessary?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Question: Can I keep my existing mixing valve and drain when installing a brand-new shower?\n\nAnswer: While it is physically possible to leave old plumbing components in place, keeping your original mixing valve and drain assembly during a full shower remodel is usually a risky shortcut that leads to premature repairs.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice:\n- Valve age and valve body condition: If your current valve is more than ten to fifteen years old, replacement cartridges may be discontinued. Swapping the rough-in valve while walls are already stripped to the studs is fast and inexpensive compared to tearing out new tile later.\n- Anti-scald safety regulations: Modern shower valves feature pressure-balancing or thermostatic technology to prevent scalding when toilets flush elsewhere in the house—an essential safety feature that older valves lack.\n- Drain size requirements: Many older tub-shower combinations utilize a 1.5-inch drain line, whereas modern walk-in showers frequently require a 2-inch drain to handle high-volume showerheads and prevent standing water on the pan.\n- Material corrosion: Brass and copper connections degrade over decades. Inspecting solder joints while the wall cavity is open prevents hidden pinhole leaks inside freshly drywalled framing.\n\nWhenever you plan a shower replacement, upgrading the rough plumbing is cheap insurance. HH Remodeling LLC coordinates plumbing sub-trades alongside master tile installation. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to inspect your bathroom layout.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}